<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     COMMON MISTAKES
     ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<section class="content-section" aria-labelledby="mistakes-heading">
  <h2 id="mistakes-heading">Common Mistakes When Converting GMT to SST</h2>
  
  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 1: Confusing American Samoa (SST, UTC-11) with Independent Samoa (WST, UTC+13)</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Independent Samoa operates on West Samoa Time (UTC+13) on the western side of the International Date Line. American Samoa operates on SST (UTC-11) on the eastern side. They are 24 hours apart in clock date!
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 2: Forgetting British Summer Time (BST) Shifts</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      GMT stays at UTC+0. However, during UK summer (late March to late October), London shifts to BST (UTC+1), expanding the time difference between London and Pago Pago from 11 hours to 12 hours.
    </p>
  </article>

  <article style="margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">
    <h3 style="font-family: 'Space Grotesk', sans-serif; font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--text);">Mistake 3: Date Boundary Miscalculations</h3>
    <p style="color: var(--muted); line-height: 1.7;">
      Converting GMT morning timestamps (before 11:00 AM GMT) rolls back to the previous day in SST. For example, 4:00 AM GMT on Thursday morning is 5:00 PM SST on Wednesday afternoon.
    </p>
  </article>
</section>

